ctually, let me think through the supply factory more carefully. It costs 200 gold and 12 wood, then produces 2 supply per turn while consuming 1 grain and 1 ore. That eliminates 11.6 gold per turn in supply purchases, but the real value is the inputs—my grain and ore are currently worthless at 1.0 and 2.8, so converting them into supply worth 5.8 each is essentially creating value from nothing.\n\nThe payback period is around 29 turns, which is borderline, but the strategic advantage of being immune to supply price spikes matters. Now I'm wondering if there's something better—like the gold mine at 沃坝 that generates 10 gold per turn with a 30-turn payback, though that requires 300 gold upfront which I don't have.\n\nLet me reconsider the economics: if I sell my grain at 1.0, that's only 4 gold per turn, but converting it through the supply factory to 2 supplies at 5.8 each gives me 11.6 gold per turn value instead. That's a massive multiplier on the same resource, which makes the supply factory look much more attractive than I initially thought.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the factory actually makes sense given my army size. With 2 armies consuming 2 supplies per turn and the factory producing 2, it's a perfect match. Three armies would need 3 supplies, so the factory wouldn't cover everything. So my plan is to build the supply factory at 新丘 for 200 gold and 12 wood, then follow it with the gold mine at 沃坝 for 300 gold and 10 wood, and finally develop the oil fields.\n\nFor the next few turns, I need to accumulate 150 more gold while maintaining 2 armies and selling off surplus resources. I'm planning to sell 6 wood and 4 ore to generate about 22 gold per turn, which should get me there in roughly 10-12 turns. I also need to revise my strategy document this turn since it's been 10 turns since the last update.",
