stocks are pure buffers. Selling 3 ore = 9 gold now, and the flow still works. Buffer risk: if my food or ore production is disrupted (e.g., I sell food below 1), the 补给厂 stops. I'll keep food ≥ 2.\n   \n   OK, T14 plan:\n   1. sell 粮食 10 → at 1.4, 10 units → price drops to ~1.2 → revenue ~13.5. Hmm. Let me sell 8 → ~11.5.\n      Actually, since I'm liquidating for acceleration, let me sell 8 food and keep 4 (flow +5/turn covers 补给厂).\n   2. sell 木头 4 → ~7.5 (price 1.9 → 1.8). Keep 4 wood (need 10 for the gold mine; wood income +3/turn net).\n   3. buy 补给 1 (5.6).\n   4. attack (32,22)P with [1,2,3].\n   \n   Cash: 76 + 11.5 + 7.5 - 5.6 ≈ 89.\n   \n   Then T15: sell 5 food (7) + 3 wood (5.5) → ~101; buy 1 supply → 95; attack another tile.\n   T16: ~108... T17 118, T18 128, T19 138, T20 148, T21 158, T22 168, T23 178, T24 188, T25 198, T26 208 → gold mine at T26. Hmm, still slow. ~10/turn.\n   \n   That's the reality: my income is ~10/turn net. \n   \n   Hmm, can I do better? Let me reconsider the food situation: my food income is 5/turn from 5 farms. Food price is depressed BY MY OWN SALES. If I sell 0 food for 3-4 turns, the price recovers to ~2.0, and my food stock grows to ~25 → then sell 25 at 1.9 (crashing to ~1.5) = ~42 gold. That's better than 10/turn... no wait, 25 food sold over 5 turns at ~1.7 avg = 42 gold = 8.4/turn. Same thing. The price impact is roughly a fixed \"loss\" per unit dumped.\n   \n   The real lever would be MORE production (more farms/lumber), but that requires cash (60/farm → 1.4-2 gold/turn = 30-40 turn payback). Not while I'm targeting the 5%/turn gold mine.\n   \n   So: my growth is capped at ~10/turn until the gold mine. The gold mine arrives ~T26. Then income 20/turn → 2nd gold mine? No more gold deposits (only 凤垒 has gold x1, already used for 1 mine). Hmm.\n   \n   So after the gold mine: 20/turn → then build mines (80 → 3.5/turn) and farms... slow compounding.\n   \n   Alternatively, think about WAR: 齐's land might have gold. If I have 3 armies + a good economy... War costs: armies, supplies, and risk. But conquering 齐 (5+ tiles) would give me their land and possibly gold deposits + their treasury (do I loot gold from captured tiles? Not stated — probably not directly; captured tiles give production). Hmm, unclear benefit.\n   \n   Actually, here's a cheaper thought: 秦's 沃坝 (mountain) had 矿场×2 → they have 2 ore mines. Not gold.\n   \n   Let me also consider: maybe I'm overcomplicating. The game has no preset objective; growth is good but so is survival/comfort. Let me just be efficient: hoard for the gold mine, keep expanding cheaply, and stay alert for 齐.\n   \n   One more consideration: should I reduce the army count to stop the supply bleed? 3 armies × 1 supply = 3/turn vs 2 produced → 5.6/turn cash cost. That's over half my net income! If I had 2 armies, I'd be supply-neutral and my savings rate would jump to ~15/turn → gold mine at ~T19-20 instead of T26! \n   \n   But how to reduce? Starve one army: it loses 10HP/turn. From 100HP → dead in 10 turns. Meanwhile it still consumes... hmm, does an under-supplied army consume the available supply? The engine allocates supply to armies; if short, some go hungry. So starving #3 means: 2 fed + 1 hungry → 10 turns of -10HP → #3 dies. Cost: 10 turns × 10HP... and #3 is my 3rd army (needed for 1-kill-per-turn expansion). With 2 armies, expansion slows (2 armies = 100×roll damage vs 100HP primitive → often a 2-round fight, so ~1 tile per 1.5-2 turns).\n   \n   Trade-off: killing #3 saves 5.6/turn (≈ 56 gold over 10 turns) and accelerates the gold mine by ~6 turns (+60 gold of mine income). Roughly break-even, and it permanently weakens my army. Not worth the complexity/risk. Keep 3 armies.\n   \n   Hmm, alternatively: keep 3 armies but let them \"starve\" partially — i.e., buy supplies only when HP matters. Since armies at 100HP can absorb -10HP/turn for several turns without real risk, I could skip buying supplies most turns and only buy when HP is