            "reasoning_content": "Moving into turn 2 now, with 1083 gold and 35 wood in reserves. I'm continuing construction across my territories—adding a second farm at 望邑, and working through 光崖's development with either a farm or a lumber energy facility, though I need to figure out the building sequence since the barracks won't be ready until turn 4.\n\nFor the other sites, I'm weighing income optimization against resource needs: 珠苑 gets another mine for quick payback, 永渚 needs lumber to sustain my wood supply since I'm burning through reserves faster than production can replace them, and 兴礁's mine is solid but I'm reconsidering whether the oil well there might be worth it for future equipment production.\n\nLooking at the bigger picture though, I realize I have 1500 gold sitting idle and could actually accelerate my military timeline by buying equipment and food from the market instead of waiting for farms to produce them. If I purchase 10 equipment and additional food now, I could recruit two armies by turn 5 instead of spreading it across turns 5 and 6, which lets me start clearing barbarian tiles immediately.\n\nFor intelligence gathering, I'm torn on whether to spy on 秦 this turn. The cost is 100 gold, which is steep, but knowing their position and expansion direction early would be crucial for deciding which tiles to prioritize. Since both 秦 and 楚 are potential rivals competing for the same 16 barbarian tiles, I should probably spy on both eventually. For now, I'll focus on 秦 since they're likely the nearer threat, and I can reassess next turn based on what I learn.\n\nOn the building front, I'm reconsidering 光崖's construction. The plan called for a 木材能源厂 there to support the 兵营 power needs, but the wood consumption (1 wood per turn) doesn't pay for itself given my current wood income. I'm thinking a 农场 might be better for food production instead, or I need to figure out if the 兵营 actually requires the power plant to function or if it can operate without it.\n\nActually, the power plant is essential — without electricity, the 兵营 stops producing entirely. So I need to build it at 光崖 this turn, which means I'll need to maintain a steady wood supply through 林场 buildings. I'm also weighing whether to build a second power plant later to support the 补给厂 and 装备厂, but those facilities have thin margins and require careful resource management.\n\nFor now, I'll focus on buying equipment directly from the market instead of building the 装备厂 — it's simpler and probably cheaper. I should also stockpile supplies since the army will consume them each turn, so I'll buy around 20 supplies this turn and more later as needed.\n\nMy building plan is taking shape: 望邑 gets the second farm, 光崖 gets the power plant, 珠苑 gets the first mine, 永渚 gets the second lumber camp, and 兴礁 gets the second mine. I'm also considering whether to add more farms at 望邑 to support the military buildup.\n\nOn food strategy, I'm realizing that buying food is roughly equivalent to building farms in the short term, but farms are better long-term since I'll need food for multiple armies. Since food prices are stable at 2.0 per unit, I'll buy the initial 10 food now and build 3-4 farms to sustain ongoing military growth.\n\nMy immediate actions are to execute the 5 builds, buy 10 food and 15 equipment, purchase 20 supplies, and spy on 秦. I need to check if I have enough wood for all the builds first.",
